        {
          "id": "msg_1778243500763_e6pxhfvwx",
          "role": "PATIENT",
          "content": "Oh, that's a tough question to pinpoint an exact start, doctor. It wasn't sudden at all. It feels like these things have been with me for as long as I can remember, really.\n\nEven as a kid, I was always known as the \"clumsy\" one, or the \"double-jointed\" kid at school. My joints, especially my knees and shoulders, would pop out of place sometimes, and I'd bruise just from leaning against a desk or bumping into something lightly. My skin always felt... different, too; remarkably stretchy, almost fragile, my mother used to say.\n\nBut it’s definitely gotten worse with age. In my 20s and 30s, the dislocations became much more frequent and painful, and that constant, nagging pain in my joints really started to set in. Now, at 51, it’s a daily struggle to manage. So, to answer your question, it's been a very gradual progression, starting in childhood, and it's just slowly compounded over the decades.",
          "isIntervention": false,
